The statement is **false** in general. A composition series records the simple composition factors of $X$, but $X$ need not be the direct sum of those factors. Below is a counterexample and the reason it fails.

---

**Counterexample.**

Let $C$ be the category of finitely generated abelian groups (equivalently, finitely generated $\mathbb{Z}$-modules). This is an abelian category. Fix a prime $p$ and take

$$X = \mathbb{Z}/p^{2}\mathbb{Z}.$$

This object has finite length. Indeed, it admits the composition series

$$0 = X_{0} \;<\; X_{1} \;<\; X_{2} = X,$$

where $X_{1} = p\mathbb{Z}/p^{2}\mathbb{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{Z}/p^{2}\mathbb{Z}$. The successive quotients are

$$X_{1}/X_{0} = X_{1} \cong \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}, \qquad X_{2}/X_{1} = (\mathbb{Z}/p^{2}\mathbb{Z})/(p\mathbb{Z}/p^{2}\mathbb{Z}) \cong \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z},$$

both of which are simple (the simple objects in this category are exactly $\mathbb{Z}/q\mathbb{Z}$ for primes $q$). So this is a valid composition series of length $n=2$.

If the claimed decomposition held, we would have

$$X \;\cong\; X_{0}\oplus X_{1}/X_{0}\oplus X_{2}/X_{1} \;=\; 0\oplus\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}\oplus\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z} \;\cong\; (\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})^{2}.$$

But $\mathbb{Z}/p^{2}\mathbb{Z}\not\cong(\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})^{2}$, since the former contains an element of order $p^{2}$ (namely the class of $1$), whereas every nonzero element of $(\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})^{2}$ has order $p$. Hence the decomposition fails.

---

**Why it fails (structural reason).**

A composition series is a filtration, not a splitting. The short exact sequences

$$0\to X_{i-1}\to X_{i}\to X_{i}/X_{i-1}\to 0$$

need not split, so $X_{i}$ need not be $X_{i-1}\oplus X_{i}/X_{i-1}$. The object $X$ is a direct sum of its composition factors precisely when $X$ is **semisimple** (equivalently, when every short exact sequence $0\to A\to X\to B\to 0$ splits, or when the Jordan–Hölder factors can be reassembled without extension data). The group $\mathbb{Z}/p^{2}\mathbb{Z}$ is a nontrivial extension of $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ by $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, so it is not semisimple, and the extension data is exactly what the direct-sum decomposition discards.

Thus the answer to the question is **no**: in general

$$X \;\not\cong\; X_{0}\oplus X_{1}/X_{0}\oplus\cdots\oplus X_{n}/X_{n-1}.$$

The equality holds if and only if $X$ is semisimple (i.e., is a direct sum of simple objects).

### PROOF COMPLETE
